WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Ninja Gaiden 2, The Dark Sword of Chaos.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:28.180
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.220
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.300
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:44.360
Ninja Gaiden 2, The Dark Sword of Chaos, has all the makings of a Hollywood-style sequel with the interesting yet vague subtitle underneath the original game.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:58.060
The cool poster, I don't know if you guys saw the art for Ninja Gaiden 2, but it has like, you know, prolific character, front and center, awesome text, giant dragon behind him.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.600
I can only imagine, honestly, with how the art style of this game.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.700
And then there's also like, you know, there's the hype factor, right?

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.360
Like Ninja Gaiden, we all love that game, right?

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.580
We all still love that game?

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.620
I did enjoy that game.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:15.920
Yeah, I mean, I haven't played it in a long time, but I still think I love that game, based on what I remember.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.180
Now, we recorded Ninja Gaiden, because it's funny Joe's saying as we remember.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:29.600
We recorded that as like episode 170, which was, geez, I can't even, is that like 2020, 2021?

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:37.920
But it's really only been a year and two months between the release of Ninja Gaiden and Ninja Gaiden 2.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:49.160
So even though it feels long for us because we play every single game, for players, you know, in real time, it wasn't that long of a wait for this sequel.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.220
So Ninja Gaiden is fresh in their mind.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:02:01.680
I'm wondering how you guys remember or think of Ninja Gaiden before going into Ninja Gaiden 2.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.340
Were you expecting, you know, we've had sequels where they just completely changed the game, right?

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.560
Ninja Gaiden 2 could have been an RPG or, you know, something where a puzzle style game.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.760
Who knows what it could have been?

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.100
It happens to just closely follow the format of Ninja Gaiden.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.280
But as we saw with Castlevania, you can't always just expect that.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:36.780
Well, joking aside, I feel like normally when we have a sequel or something, like even if we just played the game six months ago, I'm like, man, I don't remember that game.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.620
We play so many games and I just have a terrible memory.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:48.800
But Ninja Gaiden had the exact, yeah, it had the exact opposite effect for me, though, because I was like, so I was actually, I wanted to remember, like, what did we all say?

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.620
Was it, you know, did everybody voted essential and whatnot?

00:02:51.620 --> 00:03:02.860
So I went back to look at that episode and I couldn't believe how long ago it was because that one is the only one ever that feels like that was way more recent than it actually was.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.980
Like, I, you know, I'm joking, they're like, oh, like how much I remember.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.440
But like, yeah, there are things I forgot.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:19.100
But like, I do remember that like so much more than a lot of other games or at least like the main gameplay style and like the feel I got from it.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.320
And obviously, like the crazy cut scenes and all that.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:27.600
So I was, I was just surprised at how recent it was or how not recent it was and it still felt recent.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.940
Yeah, from what I remember, I really dug the presentation.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:35.820
I enjoyed the feel of the character.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:46.480
I probably, I want to say that it probably feels exactly the same, like in terms of like character movement and all that.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:54.240
But that I'm, if I were a responsible podcaster, I would have gone back and at least played a level or two just to refresh my memory.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.900
But I'm not that.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.900
Well, without getting into any of the new features, right, you have to add stuff to a second game.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.780
You can't just, well, some games do it.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:05.560
They just say like now with more levels, right?

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.520
I think in one of our sequels and spinoff sections, I think it was for Lode Runner.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:16.140
It was just filled with games where it was like, yeah, same exact controls, but we added 48 new Lode Runner levels for you.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:19.060
So, you know, you don't usually get away with that.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.560
And Ninja Gaiden 2 wasn't going to try that.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:33.140
But yeah, it is basically, you're right, Sean, it is Ninja Gaiden 1 with new abilities and new gimmicks on the levels and obviously a whole new story.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:39.180
But it's not like they didn't change even the sprite, right?

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:45.780
Like it's not like we saw a size change or a layout change or the way that Ryu controls.

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:58.560
The one big change is that in Ninja Gaiden 1, yeah, you could latch onto the walls and kind of jump from wall to wall, but you only could hang in your place.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.040
You couldn't climb up and down.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.880
Yeah, now you can actually climb up and down these walls.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:09.280
And, you know, that's like, is that a selling point?

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.260
Like that's something to talk about in this episode and feels unique.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:17.820
But do you think when they were making this game, they were like, yeah, we got to throw that like as the marketing.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.680
Like now you can climb the walls.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:25.020
It just feels like an obvious, like when you go back to the first one, you're like, huh, can't believe you couldn't climb these walls.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:34.360
Well, it does give you a bit more like a feeling of freedom of movement and you actually having like a direct relationship with this character.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:41.400
I would imagine that if I had remembered that you couldn't climb up the walls in the first one.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.280
But I would have found it a big plus.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:06:01.400
Yeah, and I have a lot of thoughts about like about the general feeling that that I feel like most people sometimes including myself have about game sequels that don't do a ton to change up their formula.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.180
Like I think that there is a place for that.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:12.560
And I guess I feel like that place occurs more often than I think most people believe.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.580
You know, I'm saying my opinion is that there's more room for that than I think most people feel like there's room for.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:23.140
Because like, you know, I've talked about this about Pokemon before where like a lot of people are like, well, when are they going to change the formula?

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:29.660
And like I'm just perfectly OK with like, hey, take this formula I love, add some new Pokemon, put me in a new region.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.400
But like you don't have to change the formula.

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:35.720
And more recently, Spider-Man 2 on the or whatever.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:37.860
Yeah, I guess it's Spider-Man 2 on the PS5.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:44.220
A lot of people were complaining where it's like, well, you know, don't expect this to be like some big formula changing thing.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:45.640
That was a great formula.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.200
And they added some nice new things.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.200
They added a couple new things.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:49.120
It was a great new story.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:54.500
And like I'm happy to get like a sequel like that that like didn't try too much to change it.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.060
Because a lot of times they try to change things.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.420
And I'm like, well, this isn't as good anymore.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:06:59.860
Or it's like not even that it's not as good.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.500
But it's like it's not what I was looking for.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.660
I'm not saying that like that's always the case.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.740
There are plenty of times like, OK, this has gotten stale or like you didn't do anything.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.620
But like I think that we'll probably get into it.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.520
I'm sure. But like there are a lot of instances in Ninja Gaiden 2 where I think it walks the line really well.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:19.180
Like one being being able to climb up those things, the walls rather than just jump on it.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.000
Where it doesn't completely change the formula.

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:32.400
It just adds a little bit more nuance and just gives you like, hey, it's another chance to use a couple more tools to explore this world or to spend time in this game or a similar game.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.040
Joe, you mentioned Spider-Man 2, which is definitely more recent in gaming history.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:43.460
But also recently on this show, I feel like Adventures of Lolo 2, you made the same defense there.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:53.640
Maybe the lone defender of saying like, hey, it's totally fine that they didn't like give Lolo like some new power that made it a new way to solve puzzles.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.700
It was totally fine to just say like, yep, first game had a great puzzle solving mechanic.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:03.800
And now we're throwing more puzzles at you, obviously two totally different games, but it's not unheard of.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:14.120
And I think especially when you go back and listen to our comments on the first Ninja Gaiden, we had basically no complaints about reuse controls and thought it was the best part of the game.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:16.160
So why screw that up?

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:20.420
Right. Like why change how he jumps or how he attacks?

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.860
Right. I don't think that that's where you need to make the adjustments.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:32.820
And so if the game starts off and it feels familiar for a game that's on the essential games list, that's a plus, right?

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:42.840
Yeah. I mean, like when a game really feels like it nails its formula and when sequels come out,

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:53.360
when sequels come out, like you kind of want them to be iterative and mostly they are like a good example of this that I was thinking of when you guys were talking is like Tony Hawk.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.200
Like the first one came out.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:57.580
It's pretty bare bones, but like the formula is there.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:01.980
It every time it releases a new one, like Tony Hawk's Pro Skater 2.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.740
Now you can like combos are easier.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.140
I don't remember exactly how.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:17.280
Maybe it's because you can manual out of out of tricks now, but like then in three, like then they introduced the reverts and I can get these super cool combos.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:26.180
But the game from one to three is exactly the same plus or minus a couple different like move concepts.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:40.680
And I think that in games that rely a lot on their, like on like that core gameplay loop, that's really all you need is just something additive, something minutely additive sometimes.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:44.480
And it seems that that might be what we're seeing here.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:50.820
Yeah. And like with something like Tony Hawk too, it's, it's, it is almost the same except for those little additive things.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:58.640
But also you are taking that formula and you're exploring new, you know, skate parks and you're exploring new areas to do.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:02.700
It's like you, you get that thing that you love doing and you get to go explore somewhere else with it.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:06.740
Like that's like, that's a totally fine way to make a sequel.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:10.680
You know, I even think about like just games that I talk about all the time, like Kingdom Hearts, love Kingdom Hearts.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.280
And like, if you take Kingdom Hearts one, you take exactly that engine, exactly that thing.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:17.160
And you just like put new worlds and gave me new stories to explore.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:25.040
Like I'm happy, you know, rather than like changing it so much that I'm like, okay, it's the same characters, but this feels like a different game now.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:37.740
You know, I mean, both can be good, but I'm just saying like, I guess my, like my whole initial point is that like, it's not, it's not an immediate negative to me to be like, well, they didn't really change that much in like the formula.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:54.760
And climbing the walls may be obvious and unmarketable in my opinion, but you can't ignore something like being able to clone yourself and have those clones run around screen and perform the same actions as you.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:59.140
That is a completely marketable, totally unique.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:02.320
I've never seen this in an NES game yet.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.540
This is awesome.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:08.800
This is the, this is the cutting edge feature, the sellable feature of this game.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:12.400
The thing that makes it worthy of the Ninja Gaiden 2.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:21.960
In fact, it'd be cool if you were only able to split your body once and the two was in reference to the two Ryus, but save the third split for the third game.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:27.420
This is, this is definitely the thing that probably warrants its own section.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.320
So let's have it out here.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:37.380
Uh, essentially there is a power up that you collect that allows Ryu to, I believe the game describes it as split his body.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.560
So it's not like a shadow version of himself or whatever.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:42.340
It's just another clone.

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:51.020
And, uh, this clone just mimics all of your inputs, including wherever you walk to, whenever you attack.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:56.560
And so, you know, it's a skill barrier.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:07.280
I want to say on the player to make these clones useful because they either can just be something that's constantly behind you, always lagging behind and not to your benefit.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:16.800
Or they can be something that you thoughtfully choose to put first and, uh, keep yourself out of harm's way to allow the clones to do the grunt work.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:22.640
Probably requires a little more time and effort and thinking, but the payoff is ultimately worth it.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:26.140
Uh, Sean, what did you think of the attack of the clones?

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:32.660
Yeah, there, um, my first thought was that they're, they're a little jank.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:54.320
Um, I think that the, the fact that, and that also makes it feel a bit more reactive and less like, cause we've, we've played games where you control more than one thing, but they're usually like moving in formation and it's very rigid and it's like two people holding a stick.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:13:03.980
So like they can't get any closer or farther away and, um, it's useful, but it doesn't really feel like revolutionary in this one.

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:10.420
Like, I don't know if they just have different inputs, like the reacting differently.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:15.340
Like, so it's always going to lag behind you and, and do exactly what you did.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:21.000
However many like ticks prior, but sometimes like the jump is sort of out of sync.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.720
And when you attack, it's definitely exactly when you attack.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:36.700
So you're sort of attacking in concert there, but there's just enough like smushiness to make it feel like something you kind of, you really have to learn and get the feel for.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:38.240
So I like it.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.560
I can't say that I was always very good at it.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:46.860
Um, there were a couple aha moments during some bosses that were really, really annoying.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:14:00.940
Um, that, oh, I just sort of lucked my way into kind of stun locking the boss, um, because it would retreat to safety where I just so happened to have one of my doppelgangers, uh, planted.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:08.780
Um, and it's kind of hard to set up because you've got so many things flying at you and everything gives you so much knockback with that.

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:11.180
That's conversation for another time.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.660
So yeah, I like the concept.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:17.820
I don't, I can't say that it's a perfect execution, but yeah, it's cool.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:23.680
Yeah. I felt like it had its, its moments where it was like, this is where it's really valuable.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:27.260
One, the one that stands out to me is a, a boss kind of early on.

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:37.440
I think it's like maybe the third or fourth boss, um, that like flies kind of overhead back and forth and you have to, you, yeah, you have to hit it to, to, to get them to come down.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.260
And then, you know, you have to hit it in the air basically.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:48.560
But like, yeah, my strategy just became after a while, I'm like, I don't know how I would have done this if I didn't have the power up where I had, you know, one or two guys behind me,

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:58.520
but I would jump in the air and land while leaving one of my other guys up in the air because they're not moving based on like what the time you were there.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:01.640
They're moving based on like how far away from that moment you are.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:02.760
That makes any sense.

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:04.200
So it's like you stand still.

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:07.100
They'll also stand still in the air behind you where you were.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:17.620
And then I'm then waiting until the boss gets near hitting him with that guy, then getting out of the way and like going immediately to jump and set up another guy in the air for when like the time comes that he's going to be in the air and that side.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:24.360
And that one like actually to me started to feel like, wow, like I'm doing this.

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:26.020
It's not like an accident.

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:36.820
But like most of the other time I was using those guys, it was like, I'm just fighting as Ryu and I'll probably get some lucky hits in with these other guys that are swinging around behind me too.

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:39.660
That was like my main strategy with those guys.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:16:02.140
Another value that they have that's a bit more apparent and easy to just immediately take advantage of is the stacking potential of your other power ups where something that might not be entirely useful at first now is just this kind of huge area of effect attack that's coming out of any duplicate that you have.

00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:05.320
So it also it also it also plays well there.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:10.140
It's definitely something that looks cooler than it performs in game.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:18.280
I think there's no doubt about that, that it's surprising when it first happens and you realize you have control over these shadow reuse.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:33.780
However, pulling it off in the game to to great effect does take the kind of maneuvering that Joe was mentioning where you kind of need to get it in the right place for it to be useful and hope to park it there in a way.

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:40.320
Otherwise, they do wind up just kind of running about the screen and nonsensically sometimes hitting things for you.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:53.200
The trouble with the clones, though, is that the game isn't treating them like a ability that Ryu always has and kind of like a metered thing that he can control.

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:56.220
But, you know, deplete over time.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:17:02.460
It's treating it as a collectible like any other power up that if you die will go away.

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:10.660
And so later on in this game, just like the first Ninja Gaiden, this game gets incredibly difficult later on.

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:25.520
But in a way that I almost feel like it expected you to have those clones to to be able to take on these bosses, just given how how large their spread of fire is and avoiding them is damn near impossible.

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:27.640
Like to do it on your own.

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:29.000
I couldn't fathom.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:33.420
So the issue becomes almost shmup like.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:37.040
Yeah. The greatest greatest paradox.

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:44.540
Yes, exactly. Like if you die and you lose all your power ups and you lose all your clones in this Ninja Gaiden game, you're suddenly a lot.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:55.000
You know, you're in a worse off situation because you're in a lot more trouble now that you are basically going to die earlier because there's a lot more that you can't deal with.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:18:08.280
I think being able to bring the clones into a situation, not just the boss fights, but being able to think about how to use them to dodge enemy attacks is where they excel best.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:14.240
And so I really think they they should have included this as a Ryu default ability.

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:24.880
And again, something that's depletable instead of something that is acquired through the level, just like your Ninja Stars or your Firewheel, etc.

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:26.340
Yeah, I agree.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.520
This this really is a hard game.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:45.000
I don't remember how because I know that Ninja Gaiden as a series is known as being hard games, but I can't remember how much of an impact that had on our our episode for the first one.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:19:01.720
But I really felt it here like I was getting actually frustrated at times, not even against bosses, but just in the scope of the normal gameplay and advocating these levels and just getting stuck in realizing that like I'm just bashing my head against a wall.

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:06.240
I need to go a little slower or not slower, but more methodical.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:12.640
And yeah, I did definitely I felt definitely felt a punishment in this game.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:24.240
Yeah, it felt to me like a game like a lot of NES games that you have to you really have to like learn, but like not not in the way where you have to like learn and memorize the stage.

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:35.640
You have to like learn a lot of like enemy behaviors and or I guess defenses against certain enemies like there are there were certain people that would just kill me over and over again.

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:42.000
As soon as I would see like one of those big guys with their swords like held out running at me, I'm like, I just don't even know how to avoid those guys.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:53.780
And I would have to like literally sit stop and like make a point to be like, OK, I got to literally just spend this level just figuring out like how do I deal with these guys, you know, and then like slowly, you know, I didn't beat this game.

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:56.040
I got maybe halfway through.

00:19:56.200 --> 00:20:05.160
So like, you know, given more time, I feel like I would like slowly, you know, as I'm saying this, this just sounds so obvious.

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:07.200
This is like literally how anyone does anything.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.840
But like, yeah, get better at it as I learned.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:18.340
But like, you know, it's like it's I guess it's just like the enemies aren't just like, oh, a different sprite in like they're doing different damage.

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:29.500
Like they do have these behaviors that I felt like I needed to not memorize when they're going to come or the layout, but like memorize like, OK, so if I see this guy, I got to react this way or I got to be quick and get out of the way, get behind them or, you know, whatever.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:36.180
Yeah, there were I felt that there were times where I was actually fighting the levels themselves.

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:39.940
Like it was never really about the enemies is about the geometry.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:44.440
And my biggest peeve, I know I'm getting a little off topic here.

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:59.420
My biggest peeve with this game, at least in its level design, is that there are some ledges that are not meant to be hopped, that there are some where you can climb to just below where you think that you could do like a little like midair.

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:03.060
Jump over and you just never will.

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:12.220
And I just like would I would just like a moth to a flame, get stuck there trying and trying to get over it.

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:22.220
And these little like flames that slowly home towards you are just are just swarming me and the other enemies are jumping around.

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.180
I'm getting knocked around back and forth.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:26.080
It actually felt like hell.

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:29.200
But I think that just because I wasn't playing the game correctly.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:31.980
Well, I mean, I think I had the exact same experience.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:35.080
And just to be clear, to make sure we're talking about the same thing.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:41.540
You're saying like when you climb out, you grab onto a wall, you climb to the top and you think you should be able to jump up onto the top and start walking.

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:44.500
And it's like, nope, you cannot go any higher than this.

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:50.700
Like, even though you can see the top right there and any person that could climb a wall should be able to pull themselves up to the top of the wall.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:53.020
But yeah, that happened to me a million times, too.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:54.480
And that was very, very frustrating.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:22:00.180
But they're like designed to not be like those are not places that you can move on from.

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:03.620
Like you're supposed to turn around or find a different route.

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:09.740
But there were some times where I felt like or I thought that you did have to get up on top of there.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:12.060
But it was like I always felt like I did.

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:16.240
Yeah. No, but I think there were times where it ended up that I did have to get up on there.

00:22:16.360 --> 00:22:17.840
But it's like you couldn't just climb up there.

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:20.820
You'd have to climb up there and then jump somewhere else to get higher.

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:22.840
So you can jump onto it from above or something.

00:22:22.840 --> 00:22:28.280
And like fine, like that's that's fine if that's the gameplay mechanic.

00:22:28.420 --> 00:22:33.140
But to me, it was just like because it looks like the other ledges that I can just jump up on.

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:36.220
And there's no contextual reason I shouldn't be able to.

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:42.320
Like it felt like it got puzzly in a way that like I wasn't prepared for a puzzle and wasn't expecting a puzzle.

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:47.840
So like that made it frustrating where I'm like, I know that like this should there should just be an easy way to get here.

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:49.700
And I can't figure it out because it's not clear.

00:22:50.360 --> 00:23:12.180
The weirdest part about the being the ability to latch on to and now scale the walls is like in the first game, if you, you know, grabbed onto a wall but not in the correct spot, you were almost like willing to accept the death or the, you know, the need to go backwards because it was like up.

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:14.120
I just didn't grab it from the right point.

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:31.920
But now that you can scale up them, it feels like specifically the pitfall ones, the ones where you have successfully dodged the pitfall but have grabbed onto the part of the wall that still sort of means death but requires you to jump to your death.

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:36.560
Like acknowledging that like, yeah, you thought you saved yourself, but you're just you're just going to have to die.

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:37.640
No two ways about it.

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:39.420
It's like it's even worse.

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:50.380
It's a worse payoff than just falling in the pit because you thought you saved yourself only to find out there's nothing you can do about the situation to Joe's point of shouldn't I just be able to pull myself up?

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:52.040
Like I don't have the upper body strength.

00:23:52.040 --> 00:23:59.200
I know that it's like a different like that there is a skill and it's it's a part of the gameplay to like, you know, jump from wall to wall.

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:07.700
And I guess having there be an animation of you just going over the ledge sort of removes from that.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:14.900
But I think in certain situations, I think it's more of a pro than a con to just like be able to climb up the corner.

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:16.760
Yeah. And onto the platform.

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:22.620
Yeah. And there are still plenty of other reasons that you could do that jump from wall to wall thing and you could need that.

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:28.640
Like it doesn't need to be like you've locked me out of simple skill.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:32.760
So I you don't have to manufacture me jumping from wall to wall.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:37.480
I'm going to jump from wall to wall to get hired, but to get to places I couldn't otherwise reach anyways.

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:45.540
So like, yeah, I had I had a similar complaint, actually, that kind of the opposite.

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:52.780
When I'm climbing down walls, sometimes like I'm climbing down something that like leads into a new area below.

00:24:53.080 --> 00:24:56.960
And like, yeah, if you fall down there, it's death, which like understandable.

00:24:56.980 --> 00:25:04.380
You fell. But like, so as long as you climb down and you get to the bottom, you touch the bottom and it like loads a new area below and you keep climbing down.

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:08.640
But then like sometimes you climb down and you get to the bottom and it's like, no, that was a pit.

00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:10.840
You died. Well, how do I know?

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:13.620
And I think like there's a ladder texture.

00:25:13.940 --> 00:25:16.360
Yeah, I think there's like, yeah, there's a texture that like gives it.

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:17.760
But like at first I didn't know.

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:19.020
So I was just like getting annoyed with that.

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:20.560
I'm like, all right, maybe I climb down here.

00:25:20.660 --> 00:25:22.480
I just touched the bottom and it was like, what?

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:24.360
What? I did do that, too, though.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:54.300
Yeah. Sean, you were mentioning the levels and perhaps describing the claustrophobic parts of the later levels, especially with all the spikes and narrow entryways with enemies.

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:59.840
But there's also the introduction of dynamic environments that Ninja Gaiden 1 was free of.

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:03.500
In Ninja Gaiden 2, there's a moving train level.

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:06.720
There's a level where the wind kind of changes directions.

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:08.900
There's an ice level.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:19.180
And, you know, again, this is the kind of stuff that you add to plus up or to justify a sequel.

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:27.280
The only complaint, you know, I hate to start with a complaint because I'm sure we can talk about good things for these dynamic environments.

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:30.760
But they they don't work with Ryu.

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:33.900
They just kind of exist at the same time as him.

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:48.420
And I feel like a good game used like a like a Super Mario Brothers creates a whole world where, like, you know, there's something about the the land that Mario runs on that changes the platforming aspect of it.

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:53.740
And instead in Ninja Gaiden 2, it's like, well, the ice level now is just like everything's slippery and it sucks more.

00:26:53.920 --> 00:27:00.060
Like, you know, if you had a hard time controlling Ryu before this, like you're really going to hate it in the ice level or with the wind changing.

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:05.920
It's like, well, you better just be more patient and wait for it to change in the direction you need.

00:27:06.020 --> 00:27:08.400
It's like it's not really like enhancing.

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:12.100
There's never it's only a hindrance to to the player character.

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:16.820
It's never like to their benefit or to pull off some really cool thing.

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:26.540
I'd say that the only one that that kind of does what you're talking about is actually the wind level, because there are times where you're going with the wind.

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:32.740
And finally, it feels like, you know, you're getting a little bit of some grace from this game.

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:37.740
But then, you know, 15 seconds later, you're fighting against it.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:39.660
The rest of them.

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:44.080
Yeah, exactly. Like they're kind of they exist separate from yourself.

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:48.640
There's the one where actually at the beginning, I was like, oh, that's really cool.

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:58.680
It's like not dynamic lighting, but like there's like a thunderstorm going on and the level keeps getting brighter and and darker.

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:01.000
And I just thought it was like a cool visual effect.

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:07.580
I'm like, oh, now I can't see where I'm jumping and there's still 15 things flying at me and I need to jump away.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:12.080
But I can't tell if that's a bottomless pit or a platform down there.

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:15.160
Guess I got to wait or just, you know, take a leap of faith.

00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:16.960
And sometimes it worked. Sometimes it didn't.

00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:22.180
But yeah, a lot of them were more like sources of frustration than like, oh, that's neat.

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:24.980
I'm I'll devil's advocate this a little bit.

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:30.200
Not in the sense that I disagree with you guys in like, yes, like Mario does this in a different way.

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:33.340
That's like it complements Mario.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:34.760
You know, it complements the way it plays.

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:37.020
Because like, yes, I agree with that.

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:43.560
But I do think that for the most part, I like they were short lived enough that they never got too frustrating to me.

00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:49.240
And I kind of appreciated this like level of challenge in like, yeah, like the wind changing one.

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:54.980
If that were if that were like became a mechanic throughout the rest of the game, I would be very annoyed.

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:59.060
But like it's just like this one level where I have to think about it this way.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:01.140
Like like that was kind of cool.

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:06.400
Same thing with the dark that the like lightning one where it's like dark and, you know, the dynamic lighting one.

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:09.620
I'm like, it's annoying.

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:15.880
Maybe. But like I actually found it kind of cool because it's like adding this level of difficulty.

00:29:16.100 --> 00:29:18.860
That's not just like, oh, because the enemies got harder.

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:23.900
And it like makes me feel like a ninja because I have to think about like, what did I see there?

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:25.020
Can I jump to that in the dark?

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:34.940
Like I got to think like the difficulty comes from me being like, I got to try and take a snapshot of this environment and like use my ninja skills to like jump around in the dark.

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:36.820
And like that felt kind of fun to me.

00:29:36.960 --> 00:29:56.340
And even like the ice, like like the ice, I think is the best example of one that like doesn't do like, you know, the ice in a Mario world, like or at least in Mario 3, like that like gives you this ability to be like, I can use the physics and like the speed that I've learned while playing this game to to like my advantage because of the ice.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.860
And like, you know, and it might be harder, but it can also be like much more satisfying.

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:08.160
And like maybe the ice level doesn't do that here, but like it still is like, okay, I'm a ninja and I got to like adapt to this new environment.

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:09.560
It's like harder now in this way.

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:15.740
Like it didn't, I really, I guess I came into this like really, I really liked these dynamic environments.

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:18.600
I thought they really added something to the game.

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:29.060
Like where it's, it's the thing that I'm like, wow, like they, they did do something that like added from the last game without over changing the formula, you know?

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:33.120
Yeah. Where, where I agree with you is on like something like the train level.

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:34.600
So it's, it's harmless.

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:46.900
It, uh, it does introduce a, you know, a side scrolling like mechanic, but, um, you know, it doesn't affect really the gameplay loop and it doesn't put the player on a, on a negative scale.

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:49.660
I'd appreciate things like that.

00:30:49.800 --> 00:31:02.120
And don't get me wrong. I'm glad that the game didn't wind up making a, a situation where it's like, you know, oh, Ryu goes into people's houses now and, and has dialogue with them to move the story along.

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:05.680
And, and, uh, you have to try to find keys or something like that.

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:11.540
Like, I'm glad that these are the kind of level changes and that it's not outright, uh, gameplay changes.

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:16.260
Like, I'm, I'm glad that they mostly just took Ninja Gaiden 1 and tried to plus it up.

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:20.920
And so, yeah, like, you know, do I hate all of those dynamic environments?

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:30.400
No. I just kind of wish whether it was through Ryu's Ninja powers or, uh, through the, through the level design, like the ice one.

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:39.300
And, and I hate to keep harping on it, but like, I wish that there was a way that like, if you knew that level, like you could actually like skate through it, right?

00:31:39.380 --> 00:31:42.820
Like you could breeze through it faster because you know the path or whatever.

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:51.240
And so it, it, it, it's good to be slippery when you know what's going on and it's bad to be slippery when you're not sure where the pitfalls are, right?

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:54.860
Like that would have been, uh, something that's more rewarding.

00:31:54.860 --> 00:32:00.720
And that probably is more rewarding to the speed runner, but it's not, it's not incredibly obvious to the player.

00:32:00.860 --> 00:32:05.280
Whereas in a Mario game, you, you know, you learn those mechanics very fast, right?

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.520
There's a, there's a lot of people in the world that are good at Mario games.

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:20.900
Yeah. I guess I'm just seeing it as like a different, it's trying to do, maybe not trying, but like, I feel like it does something different that not necessarily worse, but different than what Mario was trying to do, you know?

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:32.220
And like, not, I'm not trying to just be a contrarian, but like the, like the train one was like maybe the only one I wasn't like impressed with because like, I felt like it was just a background.

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:35.880
It didn't actually change any gameplay, you know, it was cool.

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:45.580
I was impressed with like, oh, this is a cool environment, but it wasn't until I think of the wind one where I was finally like, oh, this is actually making me think differently about how I'm approaching this level.

00:32:45.860 --> 00:32:47.280
And I think it's all a fair point.

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:48.420
I disagree.

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:53.960
Well, I don't want to be a contrarian, but that's all the, yeah, it's all to the contrary.

00:32:57.920 --> 00:33:01.000
The Ninja powers, anybody want to touch on these?

00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:08.940
Is anything like, you know, I think Sean mentioned their most useful ability is when you pair them up with the clones to kind of create these additional spreads.

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:15.220
I do hate just how limited these things are though, based on your, um, what is it?

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:17.120
Like, uh, there's, there's something you collect.

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:19.060
It's similar to the hearts in Castlevania.

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:21.340
Yeah, some kind of like energy.

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:23.360
I didn't really know how it worked, but.

00:33:23.560 --> 00:33:25.020
You just don't get to use it that often.

00:33:25.020 --> 00:33:26.660
And it has a cap on top of it.

00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:28.960
Like, I feel like the cap should go away at the least.

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:30.520
There's the cap.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:37.260
There's the fact that most of them are kind of the same thing, but in like different diagonals.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:39.940
Um, which is useful.

00:33:39.940 --> 00:33:42.140
It's just, I mean, in certain ways.

00:33:42.640 --> 00:33:45.880
Um, so like, I never really paid attention to which one I had.

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:52.220
I don't know. Maybe if I was better, maybe I would have been better at this game if I'd have actually been paying attention.

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:57.160
Um, but you know, you spam them during bosses if you've got the right direction.

00:33:57.820 --> 00:34:05.300
Uh, there's one where you kind of summon this fire circle and you're kind of invincible for a while.

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:06.700
And that's fun.

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:11.780
And then like, if you've got all of your clones, like now you're just this like conga line of destruction.

00:34:12.340 --> 00:34:16.400
Um, but yeah, other than that, I didn't really think about them too much.

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:21.620
Yeah, I, I, I wish that there was, yeah, more than just that directional thing.

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:25.880
Like the, the one you just mentioned was the one where I'm like, yeah, okay, wow, this is different and it's cool.

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.340
That little, like, that little shield that comes around you and your crew.

00:34:29.540 --> 00:34:32.060
But, but yeah, like there's throwing stars.

00:34:32.440 --> 00:34:33.560
Yeah, which all goes straight forward.

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:37.940
Then there's like fire that goes up and diagonal and then fire that goes down and diagonal.

00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:45.340
Which I just feel like the fire that goes up and diagonal is like 90% of the time way more useful than the one that goes down and diagonal.

00:34:45.520 --> 00:34:47.300
Because you're usually below enemies.

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:50.940
It does feel good to like kind of slam dunk on somebody.

00:34:51.220 --> 00:34:54.760
But it's never the most like efficient use of your time.

00:34:55.520 --> 00:34:56.400
Yeah, yeah.

00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:09.600
Somehow Ninja Gaiden 2 has even better cut scenes than what we saw in Ninja Gaiden 1.

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:10.860
Something we praised heavily.

00:35:11.140 --> 00:35:14.860
But I, I just don't know how they're squeezing the fidelity.

00:35:15.120 --> 00:35:19.960
Like these cut scenes, I can't be the only one thinking that they kind of almost, they look Super Nintendo in a way.

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:25.660
Uh, and obviously like they're doing it by using a very small portion of the screen.

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:31.840
But to their advantage, that kind of looks like it's filmed in 235.1 aspect ratio or something.

00:35:32.060 --> 00:35:34.260
Well, no, it's more like 471.

00:35:34.720 --> 00:35:35.580
Yeah, exactly.

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:37.480
It's so thin.

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:43.580
But I guess that allows them to really increase the, you know, the palette or resolution or whatever.

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:45.800
Because it really looks great.

00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:53.280
And, um, you know, this is a game, just like the first one, that you can play oblivious to the plot.

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:54.740
And it doesn't matter, right?

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:59.700
Like you're just going into the levels and you don't need any context for why you're doing anything.

00:35:59.700 --> 00:36:02.520
You're just a ninja who moves from left to right.

00:36:02.740 --> 00:36:10.080
There's no story plot points to collect or dialogue to, uh, to use later.

00:36:10.500 --> 00:36:12.320
Uh, there's no payoff like that.

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:17.320
It's just telling, uh, you know, uh, and I'm going to call it an interesting story.

00:36:17.320 --> 00:36:21.720
It's not the greatest video game story of all time, but it's definitely more thought out.

00:36:21.720 --> 00:36:24.440
More than 99% of the games we played.

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:27.020
Yeah. I, I liked it.

00:36:27.140 --> 00:36:35.740
And I liked that, you know, yes, it's very separate from the gameplay, but not, not the most separate.

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:43.120
Like, you approach a tower and then you're like, oh, I gotta climb the tower.

00:36:43.460 --> 00:36:51.740
And lo and behold, the predominant, like, movement, uh, you're going from like screen to screen in this level is vertical.

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:56.420
Upwards. And then there's one like, oh, you gotta go, uh, Hey, I'm American.

00:36:56.420 --> 00:36:57.440
I got a gun.

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:01.440
You gotta go down into the thing, uh, to go get Irene.

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:09.020
I don't know if like Irene was like a doppelganger before, but like the, the evil dude was her or was behind her.

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.560
I can't tell, but he went down.

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:14.760
So then in this level, you're going to go predominantly down.

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:19.640
So, you know, there's, there's something there.

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:21.220
Yeah. It's contextual.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:28.460
Yeah. But I, I mean, I am just like, I don't even like, I did like, I'll admit, I kind of stopped following the plot after a while.

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:35.200
Cause there's just like so many twists and turns, but I like, I am mind blown by like some of the, like that shot.

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:41.720
I think it's the tower you're talking about, Sean, the shot where like Ryu is like hanging on a cliff and like the camera, like, like tilts.

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:43.560
That's a full screen shot too.

